The problem asks for the average of $\sum_{k=1}^{100} |a_{2k-1} - a_{2k}|$ over all permutations of $\{1, 2, \ldots, 200\}$.

By linearity of expectation:
$$E\left[\sum_{k=1}^{100} |a_{2k-1} - a_{2k}|\right] = \sum_{k=1}^{100} E[|a_{2k-1} - a_{2k}|]$$

By symmetry, each pair $(a_{2k-1}, a_{2k})$ has the same expected value. So I need to find $E[|X - Y|]$ where $X$ and $Y$ are two positions in a random permutation of $\{1, 2, \ldots, 200\}$.

The key insight: over all permutations, the pair $(a_{2k-1}, a_{2k})$ is equally likely to be any ordered pair of distinct numbers from $\{1, \ldots, 200\}$. So $E[|a_{2k-1} - a_{2k}|]$ equals the average of $|i - j|$ over all ordered pairs $(i,j)$ with $i \neq j$ from $\{1, \ldots, 200\}$.

This is equivalent to finding the average over all unordered pairs, which is:
$$\frac{\sum_{1 \leq i < j \leq 200} (j-i)}{\binom{200}{2}}$$

Let me compute $\sum_{1 \leq i < j \leq 200} (j-i) = \sum_{j=2}^{200} \sum_{i=1}^{j-1} (j-i)$.

For fixed $j$: $\sum_{i=1}^{j-1} (j-i) = (j-1) + (j-2) + \cdots + 1 = \frac{(j-1)j}{2}$.

So: $\sum_{j=2}^{200} \frac{(j-1)j}{2} = \frac{1}{2}\sum_{j=2}^{200}(j^2 - j)$

$= \frac{1}{2}\left(\sum_{j=1}^{200} j^2 - \sum_{j=1}^{200} j\right)$ (since the $j=1$ terms are 0)

Using formulas:
